1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
From the beginning of time, we've battled against the very substance that keeps us alive, water.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
It's a love-hate relationship. We live by it and die by it.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Through technology and ingenuity, we're always finding new ways to stay dry.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:26,000
From the roof over our heads, to the clothing we wear and cases we carry.

5
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:31,000
From boring tunnels deep under it to building vessels that dive deep into it.

6
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:36,000
This is the amazing story of staying dry.

7
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:39,000
Now, waterproof on Modern Marbles.

8
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:53,001
marbles.

9
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:58,660
From the terrifying force of a tsunami to the insidious

10
00:00:58,661 --> 00:01:04,060
seat of a hidden leak, water can kill and destroy.

11
00:01:04,061 --> 00:01:09,060
Humans have long struggled to manage water by channeling it,

12
00:01:09,061 --> 00:01:13,060
containing it, and blocking it.

13
00:01:13,061 --> 00:01:16,060
Nowhere is this fight more evident than in one of the most

14
00:01:16,061 --> 00:01:20,060
densely populated cities in the world that's also surrounded

15
00:01:20,061 --> 00:01:22,060
by water.

16
00:01:22,061 --> 00:01:26,060
Manhattan is an island, and the fight against water here is an

17
00:01:26,061 --> 00:01:31,060
everyday reality for its 1.6 million residents.

18
00:01:31,061 --> 00:01:35,560
residents.

19
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:40,100
But it's 140 feet beneath these crowded streets that the real war

20
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:44,900
against water rages on.

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,940
In New York City's subway, water that seeps into tunnels

22
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:50,240
has always been the number one enemy.

23
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,640
We'll stop working on a Saturday morning,

24
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:55,080
and by the time we return to work on a Monday,

25
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:57,620
we'll have two to three, four feet of water in here,

26
00:01:57,720 --> 00:01:59,480
and that's without rain.

27
00:01:59,481 --> 00:02:01,980
The Metropolitan Transportation Authority

28
00:02:01,981 --> 00:02:05,380
is halfway through a massive subway expansion project.

29
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,700
We have anywhere from six to eight of these bumps

30
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:12,640
constantly running to retrieve the water,

31
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:15,380
and we put it through a pump out into the sewer lines.

32
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,080
They've dug out two new colossal tunnels down here,

33
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:24,120
and in doing so, they've put themselves

34
00:02:24,121 --> 00:02:26,760
under a watery siege 24-7.

35
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:31,760
The whole transit system that is underground primarily,

36
00:02:31,761 --> 00:02:37,760
we are every day pumping out 13 million gallons of water on a dry day.

37
00:02:37,761 --> 00:02:40,760
If you're underground, you have a force to reckon with,

38
00:02:40,761 --> 00:02:42,760
and that's hydrostatic pressure.

39
00:02:42,761 --> 00:02:46,760
The mass of water and the mass of Earth puts water under pressure.

40
00:02:46,761 --> 00:02:49,760
If you're going to build a hole in the ground like a tunnel,

41
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:55,760
then water in the ground around it can push its way through any cracks or seams

42
00:02:55,761 --> 00:02:57,660
that you have in that tunnel.

43
00:02:57,661 --> 00:03:00,540
That makes water management one of the subway's biggest

44
00:03:00,541 --> 00:03:02,040
worries.

45
00:03:02,041 --> 00:03:05,440
You dig down, let's say, 10, 20 feet,

46
00:03:05,441 --> 00:03:08,080
you start heating water.

47
00:03:08,081 --> 00:03:11,080
Peter Strasser is head of Wisco America,

48
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:13,880
a company that specializes in large-scale underground

49
00:03:13,881 --> 00:03:16,620
waterproofing.

50
00:03:16,621 --> 00:03:19,760
In the battle against water, their most powerful weapon

51
00:03:19,761 --> 00:03:22,720
is a massive continuous PVC membrane

52
00:03:22,721 --> 00:03:26,320
attaching to the new tunnel walls.

53
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:28,960
Strasser introduced this ingenious waterproofing system

54
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:33,560
to America in 1984.

55
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:35,560
Since then, they've done installations

56
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:39,340
in over 65 underground structures in the US,

57
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:43,080
including stations in the Washington DC metro.

58
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,220
But can Wisco really keep water out of the New York subway?

59
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:51,480
Leaks have plagued the system since the beginning.

60
00:03:51,481 --> 00:03:56,280
New York first began building a subway in 1900.

61
00:03:56,380 --> 00:04:01,620
The battle against drips and leaks started at the same time.

62
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:07,020
Builders tried using asphalt to keep leaks out.

63
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:10,240
New York City has a lot of subway systems

64
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:14,140
built in the 30s and 40s, you know.

65
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,980
The tunnels are leaking, all the tiles falling down,

66
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:20,980
because at that time, they didn't have the right technique

67
00:04:20,981 --> 00:04:23,940
to waterproof a tunnel.

68
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:25,980
Leakage in the subways has cost millions

69
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:29,460
of dollars in damage, in the form of rusty tracks,

70
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:35,460
electrical shortouts, and falling tile and bricks.

71
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,900
Average maintenance costs can range between

72
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:42,800
under to $500,000 a month.

73
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:45,500
It's a daunting task to keep New York's 800 miles

74
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:48,140
of track dry.

75
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,440
New York subway is one of the oldest, largest

76
00:04:50,441 --> 00:04:53,580
largest and busiest rapid transit systems in the world.

77
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:56,740
It is the circulatory system of the city,

78
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:58,980
with 4 million riders a day.

79
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:01,680
But the city is still growing,

80
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:05,180
so now it's carrying out the biggest subway expansion

81
00:05:05,181 --> 00:05:06,160
in two generations.

82
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:11,360
They're extending the No. 7 line 1.3 miles

83
00:05:11,361 --> 00:05:13,460
from Times Square to 11th Avenue,

84
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:15,400
and then south to 26th Street.

85
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,300
And on the east side, the Long Island Railroad

86
00:05:19,301 --> 00:05:22,060
will get eight new tracks, bringing riders

87
00:05:22,061 --> 00:05:24,480
to Grand Central Terminal.

88
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:26,320
With the stakes higher than ever in the battle

89
00:05:26,321 --> 00:05:29,080
to keep New York's subway dry, the city

90
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:31,320
is turning to this waterproof membrane

91
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:34,340
to prevent leakage in the new tunnels.

92
00:05:34,341 --> 00:05:36,940
The tunnel membrane is made of PVC.

93
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:38,580
You might know it as vinyl.

94
00:05:38,581 --> 00:05:40,680
This is very similar to the material that

95
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:43,000
would be used in shower curtains,

96
00:05:43,001 --> 00:05:46,760
except that it's about 10 times as thick as a shower curtain,

97
00:05:46,761 --> 00:05:51,020
but with the same flexibility and waterproof qualities.

98
00:05:51,021 --> 00:05:54,580
Waterproofing is really all about molecular stickiness.

99
00:05:54,581 --> 00:05:56,000
This is a water molecule.

100
00:05:56,001 --> 00:05:58,300
You have two hydrogens and an oxygen.

101
00:05:58,301 --> 00:06:01,400
This side of the molecule is relatively positive,

102
00:06:01,401 --> 00:06:03,680
and this side is relatively negative.

103
00:06:03,681 --> 00:06:05,440
Think of it like the poles of a magnet.

104
00:06:05,441 --> 00:06:07,420
It wants to adhere to itself.

105
00:06:07,421 --> 00:06:11,080
But as you can see, it can also want to adhere to surfaces.

106
00:06:11,081 --> 00:06:14,000
So waterproofing is like putting a barrier

107
00:06:14,001 --> 00:06:18,540
the water molecules and the surface such that they would

108
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:21,440
prefer to stick to one another.

109
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:24,980
The PVC membrane is a water barrier that can stretch,

110
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:27,140
unlike the stiffer high-density plastics

111
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,680
used in some other systems.

112
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:32,320
Here, that flexibility is expanded

113
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:35,260
by installing the membrane without ever puncturing it.

114
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:39,360
To begin, workers secure a layer of either felt or geotextile

115
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:42,160
with nails as another technology.

116
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:45,160
This layer helps drain water and provides protection

117
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:47,400
for the waterproof membrane.

118
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:53,340
Then the crew nails down the felt with its attached PVC disks.

119
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:56,940
The yellow membrane is then heat sealed to the PVC disks

120
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:00,540
instead of nailing it to the wall.

121
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:03,240
In other systems, the membrane is directly attached

122
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:06,080
to the wall with nails, so it may tear or leak

123
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:09,180
when the rock shifts.

124
00:07:09,181 --> 00:07:12,020
By welding the membrane to PVC disks,

125
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:16,420
the system has more flexibility and is watertight.

126
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:20,920
You shouldn't penetrate the actual waterproofing membrane.

127
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:23,920
So that's the reason we developed these disks,

128
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:27,340
so that there's no hole made in the membrane, nothing.

129
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:29,340
No matter the level of precaution,

130
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:32,540
leaks in the subway are always possible.

131
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:34,880
In older systems, it's hard to plug the source

132
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:37,880
because water can travel the whole length of the tunnel.

133
00:07:37,881 --> 00:07:40,880
You really go in and pump grout to keep the water out.

134
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:43,880
The problem with that is that it's a labor-intensive thing.

135
00:07:43,881 --> 00:07:46,880
You have to come over and over to do that.

136
00:07:46,881 --> 00:07:49,880
Water seeks the path of least resistance.

137
00:07:49,881 --> 00:07:51,880
If it's blocked in one part of the tunnel,

138
00:07:51,881 --> 00:07:53,880
it'll travel as far as it needs to go

139
00:07:53,881 --> 00:07:56,880
until it finds the next crack.

140
00:07:56,881 --> 00:08:00,880
You push the water further, further, further, further,

141
00:08:00,881 --> 00:08:05,880
so it can be that you have to route the complete tunnel.

142
00:08:05,881 --> 00:08:12,880
To make repairs easier, the crew installs ribbed vinyl barriers every 50 feet.

143
00:08:12,881 --> 00:08:20,880
These divide the tunnel up into individual sections, allowing for more targeted repairs from water damage.

144
00:08:20,881 --> 00:08:25,880
We know exactly the leak can only be between these two water barriers.

145
00:08:25,881 --> 00:08:32,880
Once the membrane is up, the crew erects a steel rebar grid that they welded together.

146
00:08:32,881 --> 00:08:35,880
This will serve as the reinforcement for the final

147
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:39,880
concrete structure that will complete the tunnel.

148
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:42,520
According to Wisco, this state-of-the-art system

149
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:46,120
has a 100% success rate.

150
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:49,120
New Yorkers are surrounded on all sides by water,

151
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:53,240
but they're pushing back with everything they've got.

152
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:56,140
The MTA is counting on new and better waterproofing

153
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:59,480
technology to keep more than 1.5 billion riders yearly

154
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,880
moving efficiently through the city.

155
00:09:02,881 --> 00:09:05,780
Bottom line is that the better the waterproofing,

156
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:08,480
the better the subway will be.

157
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,780
New York, without the subway, it will be another village.

158
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:15,720
Keeping their subway dry is a battle New Yorkers must win.

159
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:22,640
The modern submarine is a sleek, powerful vessel

160
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:27,640
that defies the many tons of water pressing in around it.

161
00:09:27,740 --> 00:09:31,080
Submarines carry humans and sophisticated technology

162
00:09:31,081 --> 00:09:35,080
hundreds of feet down to execute complicated missions.

163
00:09:35,081 --> 00:09:39,080
At this depth, there's no room for error.

164
00:09:39,081 --> 00:09:43,080
If a submarine's waterproofing breaks down, it's sunk.

165
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:46,080
It's extremely important to have a waterproof submarine.

166
00:09:46,081 --> 00:09:48,080
Everything has to not only be waterproof,

167
00:09:48,081 --> 00:09:50,080
it has to be pressure-proof as well.

168
00:09:50,081 --> 00:09:52,080
I'm not just going underwater and getting wet.

169
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:55,080
I'm exposing this thing to extreme pressures.

170
00:09:55,081 --> 00:09:58,080
Author Mark Reagan and his friend John Reichenberg

171
00:09:58,081 --> 00:10:01,400
are amongst a rare breed of personal submarine hobbyists

172
00:10:01,401 --> 00:10:03,800
who build and customize their own subs.

173
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:06,760
It's a ball to be in.

174
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:08,620
I mean, it just gives you the real feel

175
00:10:08,621 --> 00:10:10,960
of what everybody went through in the real submarines.

176
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:14,060
It may be small, but a personal sub

177
00:10:14,061 --> 00:10:16,560
is up against the same forces of water as a big one.

178
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,120
And its design must be just as waterproof.

179
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:24,580
Reagan built his sub from blueprints

180
00:10:24,581 --> 00:10:26,640
for a model dubbed the K-250,

181
00:10:26,641 --> 00:10:30,640
designed by Naval Captain George Kittredge in the 1970s.

182
00:10:30,641 --> 00:10:34,640
Kittredge served on submarines during World War II.

183
00:10:34,641 --> 00:10:39,640
On seven patrols, his sub torpedoed 16 enemy ships.

184
00:10:39,641 --> 00:10:43,640
At the end of the war, he was promoted to captain at age 26.

185
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:49,640
A couple of decades later,

186
00:10:49,641 --> 00:10:52,640
Kittredge began designing personal submarines.

187
00:10:52,641 --> 00:10:56,240
Today, there are an estimated 100 Kittredge-designed

188
00:10:56,241 --> 00:10:58,640
personal subs prowling the depths.

189
00:10:58,641 --> 00:11:01,280
Kittredge just died in 2010.

190
00:11:01,281 --> 00:11:04,280
He was 92 years old, and he was kicking right up to the end.

191
00:11:04,281 --> 00:11:06,680
Matter of fact, he was still dabbling with submarines

192
00:11:06,681 --> 00:11:08,280
right up to the end.

193
00:11:08,281 --> 00:11:12,720
Kittredge named this model K250 because with its original

194
00:11:12,721 --> 00:11:16,200
acrylic hatch, it could be safely operated to a depth of

195
00:11:16,201 --> 00:11:17,640
250 feet.

196
00:11:17,641 --> 00:11:22,640
But you put a steel hatch on it, it could go as much as 800 feet.

197
00:11:22,641 --> 00:11:23,400
to 1,000.

198
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:26,400
Today's nuclear subs cruise at a depth

199
00:11:26,401 --> 00:11:27,680
of at least 800 feet

200
00:11:27,681 --> 00:11:31,860
and can withstand 350 pounds of pressure per square inch.

201
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:37,000
But the history of submarines goes way back.

202
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:41,700
In the 1620s, a Dutchman named Cornelius Drebel

203
00:11:41,701 --> 00:11:44,360
invented a working sub with a shell made of wood

204
00:11:44,361 --> 00:11:45,640
and waterproof leather.

205
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:49,020
Drebel's sub could dive down 12 to 15 feet.

206
00:11:49,021 --> 00:11:52,320
Waterproofing technology made great strides,

207
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:54,720
and by the time World War II ended,

208
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:58,760
submarines could dive down to about 400 feet.

209
00:11:58,860 --> 00:12:00,760
It was dangerous work.

210
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:06,680
20% of Americans who fought in submarines never returned.

211
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:10,080
Today, thanks to advanced waterproofing methods,

212
00:12:10,180 --> 00:12:13,720
submersibles can explore ocean depths never seen before.

213
00:12:13,721 --> 00:12:14,421
seen before.

214
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:22,320
The Alvin, operated by the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institute,

215
00:12:22,321 --> 00:12:25,820
can descend to a depth of three miles.

216
00:12:25,821 --> 00:12:28,320
That's as deep as 12 Empire State Buildings stacked

217
00:12:28,321 --> 00:12:29,021
top to bottom.

218
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:34,780
No matter how deep it goes, a submarine's ability

219
00:12:34,781 --> 00:12:36,720
to withstand water pressure starts

220
00:12:36,721 --> 00:12:39,960
with exceptionally strong seals along every opening.

221
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:46,620
These are the most waterproof and the most pressure-proof

222
00:12:46,621 --> 00:12:48,100
of all the seals are for the motors.

223
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:51,460
These are actually stainless steel shafts that penetrate the hull,

224
00:12:51,660 --> 00:12:56,440
and within that shaft are two O-rings that can hold out 10,000 PSI.

225
00:12:57,140 --> 00:12:58,880
The hatch to the conning tower,

226
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:00,860
which allows the operator in and out,

227
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,500
is the largest opening that needs to be sealed.

228
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:06,900
This is the most important seal, really,

229
00:13:06,901 --> 00:13:11,000
because this seals you in with big bolting mechanisms inside

230
00:13:11,001 --> 00:13:12,260
that keeps the water out.

231
00:13:12,261 --> 00:13:15,560
The hatch is actually a little bit over a quarter inch thick.

232
00:13:15,561 --> 00:13:17,880
We've got an O-ring seal in here made out of rubber

233
00:13:17,881 --> 00:13:20,500
put into a specially lathe groove.

234
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:22,800
As the pressure goes down on it,

235
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:25,720
it'll push this O-ring into a little cavity

236
00:13:25,721 --> 00:13:28,980
that's specially designed for this to seal into

237
00:13:28,981 --> 00:13:32,400
because you've got literally hundreds of pounds of pressure

238
00:13:32,401 --> 00:13:33,340
pushing down on this.

239
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:36,060
At 10 feet deep, you wouldn't be able to open this hatch.

240
00:13:36,140 --> 00:13:37,500
It's just a little water pressure on it.

241
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:39,300
Well, let's go over the seals.

242
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:41,800
Ironically, submarines do let in water

243
00:13:41,801 --> 00:13:44,740
in order to submerge.

244
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:47,700
The operator opens a valve to exchange water for air

245
00:13:47,701 --> 00:13:50,900
in the ballast, the chamber between its inner and outer

246
00:13:50,901 --> 00:13:53,140
hull.

247
00:13:53,141 --> 00:13:54,740
Since the seawater entering the tank

248
00:13:54,741 --> 00:13:57,220
is heavier than the air it replaces,

249
00:13:57,221 --> 00:13:59,560
the submarine gets heavier and submerges.

250
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:06,360
It takes about two minutes for Mark Reagan

251
00:14:06,361 --> 00:14:11,120
to submerge his K250 15 feet into the waters of Glee Bay,

252
00:14:11,121 --> 00:14:12,660
and inlet of the Chesapeake.

253
00:14:12,661 --> 00:14:14,460
When I vent these ballast tanks,

254
00:14:14,461 --> 00:14:16,860
I become neutral in the water, just like a fish

255
00:14:16,861 --> 00:14:19,360
and rise and sink as I please.

256
00:14:19,361 --> 00:14:21,760
In this way, all submarines exemplify

257
00:14:21,761 --> 00:14:25,140
our love-hate relationship with water.

258
00:14:25,141 --> 00:14:28,200
They need water to operate, while remaining heavily armed

259
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:32,180
against its destructive force.

260
00:14:32,181 --> 00:14:36,480
The damaging power of water is unquestionable.

261
00:14:36,481 --> 00:14:39,520
In a flash, rain can ruin millions of dollars

262
00:14:39,521 --> 00:14:44,520
for the military, firefighters, and first responders.

263
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:47,220
In harsh and sometimes wet conditions,

264
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:50,060
watertight cases are essential to protect equipment.

265
00:14:52,860 --> 00:14:55,940
Pelican cases were invented in the 1970s

266
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:59,100
in the garage of a man named Dave Parker.

267
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,380
Parker was a diver who wanted rugged equipment

268
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:03,620
he could use in and around the water,

269
00:15:03,720 --> 00:15:07,280
so he started making it himself.

270
00:15:07,281 --> 00:15:10,380
Today at its headquarters in Torrance, California,

271
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:13,780
Pelican makes watertight lights and dive gear.

272
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:17,060
But it's best known for its nearly indestructible

273
00:15:17,061 --> 00:15:21,180
watertight cases, all ready to take on harsh conditions.

274
00:15:23,560 --> 00:15:26,060
It could be a hurricane, could be dropped out

275
00:15:26,061 --> 00:15:28,020
of the back of vehicles, things like that.

276
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:31,820
So the whole case is constructed to be robust

277
00:15:31,821 --> 00:15:32,900
in those environments.

278
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:35,660
The glass vase goes in.

279
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:38,540
and the case goes over.

280
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:41,680
Pelican prides itself on the toughness

281
00:15:41,681 --> 00:15:42,820
of its product testing.

282
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:48,120
Throughout manufacturing,

283
00:15:48,121 --> 00:15:49,920
workers run through patented steps

284
00:15:49,921 --> 00:15:51,460
to make the cases watertight,

285
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:55,300
with the help of their mechanical colleagues.

286
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:01,240
Domingo Acevedo has worked at Pelican for 10 years

287
00:16:01,241 --> 00:16:03,740
and supervises production on the floor.

288
00:16:03,741 --> 00:16:05,880
This is Shamu, this is 11-hundred ton machine.

289
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,180
And what you see right now coming out,

290
00:16:09,181 --> 00:16:12,360
being assembled over on this side, is the 1520 case.

291
00:16:13,320 --> 00:16:15,160
The cases are made of a plastic resin

292
00:16:15,161 --> 00:16:18,060
that's stored in silos as pellets.

293
00:16:18,061 --> 00:16:19,160
They're piped into hoppers

294
00:16:19,161 --> 00:16:21,600
where they're mixed with various colors.

295
00:16:21,601 --> 00:16:23,560
The pellets are then sent to production stations

296
00:16:23,561 --> 00:16:26,900
around the factory, where they're cooked at very high heat

297
00:16:26,901 --> 00:16:29,400
and injected into a mold or tool.

298
00:16:29,401 --> 00:16:30,540
This is a long barrel,

299
00:16:30,541 --> 00:16:33,840
and that barrel's running at about 420 degrees.

300
00:16:33,841 --> 00:16:35,640
It melts the material.

301
00:16:35,641 --> 00:16:38,260
It pushes the material inside the tool,

302
00:16:38,261 --> 00:16:40,340
and then it has a cooling period.

303
00:16:40,341 --> 00:16:43,020
Once it cools enough, the robot will come pick it up,

304
00:16:43,021 --> 00:16:44,560
drop it down on the line,

305
00:16:44,561 --> 00:16:46,800
and then everything gets assembled from there.

306
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,620
Now the workers take over to put on latches

307
00:16:50,621 --> 00:16:52,200
and the crucial O-ring,

308
00:16:52,201 --> 00:16:55,280
a neoprene band that seals the edge of the case,

309
00:16:55,281 --> 00:16:57,660
just like an O-ring seals a submarine hatch.

310
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:01,700
This makes it watertight.

311
00:17:01,701 --> 00:17:04,980
Not only the O-ring, but also the latches

312
00:17:04,981 --> 00:17:09,940
very, very important because that's what gives it the tension and the pressure to keep that O-ring

313
00:17:09,941 --> 00:17:12,170
in place to make it to make sure that it's watertight.

314
00:17:12,171 --> 00:17:13,740
We don't want to make it to where it's

315
00:17:13,741 --> 00:17:18,700
impossible to open, but we also want to make it to where if it drops, it doesn't pop open by itself.

316
00:17:19,420 --> 00:17:24,100
To make their cases even more watertight, Pelican uses a latch without springs.

317
00:17:24,780 --> 00:17:28,576
All there is is just a pin that holds the lever and the latch in place.

318
00:17:28,577 --> 00:17:30,020
You can put it in anything.

319
00:17:30,140 --> 00:17:32,007
It'll never rust. It'll never get stuck.

320
00:17:32,008 --> 00:17:34,760
And that's the reason why we don't use any springs on this.

321
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:40,280
And Pelican also uses a purge valve, which keeps the pressure inside the case equalized.

322
00:17:40,780 --> 00:17:45,140
It'll let the air go out, but it never allows the water or anything to go inside.

323
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:47,700
Say you go up in a high altitude and so forth.

324
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:50,060
If you didn't have this, your case would blow up.

325
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:52,540
Three features keep water out.

326
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:56,040
The O-rings and latches, which seal the box tight.

327
00:17:56,340 --> 00:17:59,620
And the purge valve, which keeps pressure from building up inside.

328
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,740
Vital tools are sealed in.

329
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:03,520
Water is sealed out.

330
00:18:04,980 --> 00:18:06,320
You can throw it in the ocean.

331
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:07,960
You can do whatever you want with it, you know,

332
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:09,720
and it just, it'll never leak.

333
00:18:10,680 --> 00:18:13,660
Every day, Pelican makes up to 7,000 cases.

334
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:17,000
Testing the case is an integral part of the process.

335
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:20,260
The inspectors go around the case line,

336
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:21,800
take certain cases randomly,

337
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:23,680
and then they'll bring them over here to the test lab.

338
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:25,220
And what she's doing right now

339
00:18:25,221 --> 00:18:26,520
is that she's going to check it for any kind of leaks.

340
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:29,060
The case is lined with tissue paper

341
00:18:29,061 --> 00:18:30,360
and an absorbent tape

342
00:18:30,361 --> 00:18:32,640
that will darken wherever water touches it.

343
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:34,400
That way we can actually see the trail

344
00:18:34,401 --> 00:18:36,340
and see exactly where it's coming from,

345
00:18:36,341 --> 00:18:40,380
whether from the O-ring itself or from the purge valve.

346
00:18:40,381 --> 00:18:43,480
The case is stuffed with five-pound weights.

347
00:18:43,481 --> 00:18:49,720
And after an hour of soaking, there's no sign of water.

348
00:18:49,721 --> 00:18:58,220
Pelican also tests the latches and bakes their cases,

349
00:18:58,221 --> 00:19:00,420
anything to make sure they stay dry inside.

350
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:13,280
Winning the battle against water means having the ability to take critical equipment anywhere, anytime, without fear of the weather.

351
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:22,520
Sometimes waterproofing can be a matter of life and death.

352
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:29,040
When natural disasters strike, thousands of homeless find themselves struggling to survive.

353
00:19:29,041 --> 00:19:33,440
Emergency rescue workers use tarpaulins, or tarps,

354
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:36,580
to protect supplies and provide temporary shelters.

355
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:39,580
When a natural disaster of the magnitude of a Katrina

356
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:42,380
happens, the demand and the need for protection

357
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:43,680
is tremendous.

358
00:19:43,780 --> 00:19:46,060
MyWeave gets the call because it's

359
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:48,700
the only US-based manufacturer of waterproof

360
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,400
or polyethylene tarps.

361
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:54,460
Tarp is in every corner of our lives,

362
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:59,040
a 100% waterproofing material that we take for granted.

363
00:19:59,041 --> 00:20:01,780
It's cheap, durable, and light.

364
00:20:01,880 --> 00:20:04,240
It was absolutely a perfect match for people

365
00:20:04,340 --> 00:20:07,080
that wanted temporary covers of all kinds.

366
00:20:07,180 --> 00:20:08,580
You see it on the farms.

367
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:10,780
You see it in people's backyards, boats.

368
00:20:10,880 --> 00:20:14,080
You see it just basically everywhere you go.

369
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:16,520
Long before modern plastics,

370
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:19,760
humans used tarp-like covers.

371
00:20:19,860 --> 00:20:22,360
To protect objects onboard ships,

372
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:27,760
early sailors used tarpaulins, heavy canvas treated with tar.

373
00:20:27,761 --> 00:20:31,100
Sailors also wore waterproof hats made of tarpaulin,

374
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:33,160
which is how they got the nickname, tars.

375
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:38,500
What makes modern-day tarps waterproof

376
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:43,200
is a molecule made from oil or gas, polyethylene.

377
00:20:43,300 --> 00:20:45,420
Polyethylene feels waxy.

378
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:48,320
It's long molecules that really have nothing on them

379
00:20:48,420 --> 00:20:50,420
for the water to adhere to.

380
00:20:50,520 --> 00:20:52,160
An early version of polyethylene

381
00:20:52,260 --> 00:20:55,660
was first synthesized accidentally by German physicist

382
00:20:55,661 --> 00:20:59,000
physicist Hans von Peckmann in 1898.

383
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:01,760
The Phillips Petroleum Company tried to manufacture it for

384
00:21:01,860 --> 00:21:06,140
years, but it was a financial failure until they used it to

385
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:08,040
make the hula hoop.

386
00:21:13,140 --> 00:21:16,740
To make tarps, polyethylene comes to the factory in the form

387
00:21:16,840 --> 00:21:18,380
of small pellets.

388
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:21,380
They get transported to us in rail cars.

389
00:21:21,480 --> 00:21:23,980
There's approximately 200,000 pounds in each one of these

390
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:25,260
rail cars.

391
00:21:25,261 --> 00:21:28,800
They get automatically siphoned up through this pipe system

392
00:21:28,900 --> 00:21:31,300
and put into our silos.

393
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:34,140
My weave keeps about a million pounds of polyethylene

394
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:36,440
on hand all the time.

395
00:21:36,540 --> 00:21:40,780
The pellets are piped into the factory as needed.

396
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:44,680
Making tarps requires two different types of polyethylene,

397
00:21:44,780 --> 00:21:47,680
high density to make the fabric and low density

398
00:21:47,780 --> 00:21:50,380
to make the waterproof coating.

399
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:52,480
It's this variation in density that

400
00:21:52,481 --> 00:21:54,980
that makes it the most widely used plastic in the world.

401
00:21:58,320 --> 00:22:00,720
Manufacturing tarps starts with making thread

402
00:22:00,721 --> 00:22:03,900
from pellets of high-density polyethylene.

403
00:22:03,901 --> 00:22:06,620
It's being dropped down into the extruder

404
00:22:06,621 --> 00:22:10,800
and brought up to approximately 580 degrees.

405
00:22:10,801 --> 00:22:13,160
It gets dropped down through a slot die

406
00:22:13,161 --> 00:22:15,740
into the water bath where it has to be cooled

407
00:22:15,741 --> 00:22:18,600
and turned into a film.

408
00:22:18,601 --> 00:22:21,640
The film is then sliced up into yarn.

409
00:22:21,641 --> 00:22:24,680
We do that by first slitting it through the slitter bar.

410
00:22:25,180 --> 00:22:28,300
You can see there's a series of blades all the way across here.

411
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:31,420
And then it starts to go through the orientation process,

412
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:32,980
which is what is going to give it strength.

413
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:37,900
You can actually stretch the material six and a half or so to one.

414
00:22:38,540 --> 00:22:39,880
Now that has pretty good strength.

415
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:44,580
If you don't stretch it, you can take the material and it has no strength at all.

416
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:50,660
Now the yarn is ready to be spooled and spun.

417
00:22:51,640 --> 00:22:58,000
The fiber made now, you get approximately 60,000 yards, which is very close to five miles.

418
00:22:58,580 --> 00:23:00,960
And from here on in, this is a real textile process.

419
00:23:01,900 --> 00:23:05,960
It's not much different than traditional weaving, just bigger and faster.

420
00:23:07,780 --> 00:23:14,840
Looking like a troop of spinning spiders, 1,800 spools feed their lines onto one large 12-foot beam.

421
00:23:14,841 --> 00:23:22,640
The beam feeds the automated loom that weaves at warp speed, 30 inches of fabric a minute.

422
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:30,540
The freshly woven fabric then gets waterproofed with a coating of low-density polyethylene.

423
00:23:33,220 --> 00:23:41,300
Whether black, red, or the popular blue, this waterproof coating can be made thick or thin, lightweight, or longer-lasting.

424
00:23:41,301 --> 00:23:44,300
TARP is rough and ready and can waterproof anything

425
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:48,300
from a quick repair job to a 20,000 seat stadium.

426
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:53,040
The only thing you can't do with TARP is wear it.

427
00:23:53,140 --> 00:23:55,640
Humans have always struggled to keep themselves dry

428
00:23:55,740 --> 00:23:57,540
against the elements.

429
00:23:57,640 --> 00:24:01,060
As early as the 13th century, Amazonian Indians

430
00:24:01,160 --> 00:24:03,460
used a milky substance from rubber trees

431
00:24:03,560 --> 00:24:06,560
to waterproof footwear and capes.

432
00:24:06,660 --> 00:24:11,300
In 1823, Charles McIntosh invented a method for combating

433
00:24:11,301 --> 00:24:15,140
with fabric to make the first modern raincoats.

434
00:24:15,240 --> 00:24:19,440
That's why the English still call raincoats Macintoshes.

435
00:24:19,540 --> 00:24:22,340
Similarly, the Duke of Wellington invented the Wellington boot

436
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:25,540
in the 19th century.

437
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:28,260
Rubber boots are still known as Wellingtons in England.

438
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:35,160
Despite these 19th century waterproofing milestones,

439
00:24:35,260 --> 00:24:37,760
American soldiers in the Second World War

440
00:24:37,860 --> 00:24:40,460
were not equipped with waterproof boots.

441
00:24:40,461 --> 00:24:44,760
Snow, cold rain, mud.

442
00:24:44,761 --> 00:24:47,000
These conditions lead to trench foot,

443
00:24:47,001 --> 00:24:50,300
especially when men are crouched in wet, cold foxholes

444
00:24:50,301 --> 00:24:52,300
for hours.

445
00:24:52,301 --> 00:24:55,000
Surprisingly, the boots that American troops wore

446
00:24:55,001 --> 00:24:58,820
had only rubber soles with leather tops.

447
00:24:58,821 --> 00:25:01,120
Foot problems thinned the ranks.

448
00:25:01,121 --> 00:25:04,460
On the 5th Army Front in the winter of 1944,

449
00:25:04,461 --> 00:25:07,120
the ratio of trench foot to battle casualties

450
00:25:07,121 --> 00:25:10,460
was 1 to 3 1⁄2.

451
00:25:10,461 --> 00:25:14,300
The G.I.'s developed trench foot, a frostbite infection

452
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:17,660
that led to blisters, gangrene, and even death.

453
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:23,300
Waterproofing was a serious matter for the military.

454
00:25:25,900 --> 00:25:30,120
Staying dry was a life and death issue for mountaineers as well.

455
00:25:30,220 --> 00:25:32,520
They had two choices of materials,

456
00:25:32,620 --> 00:25:35,160
a vinyl, which kept water and wind out,

457
00:25:35,260 --> 00:25:37,560
and wool, which allowed the skin to breathe

458
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:39,420
but lost its warmth when wet.

459
00:25:39,421 --> 00:25:42,260
If you go back and you look at images of mountaineers,

460
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:44,260
you know, they're covered in layer after layer

461
00:25:44,360 --> 00:25:47,160
after layer after layer of wool.

462
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:49,160
When Bob Gore discovered a substance

463
00:25:49,260 --> 00:25:52,760
that led to Gore-Tex in 1976, it

464
00:25:52,860 --> 00:25:54,860
revolutionized waterproof clothing.

465
00:25:59,680 --> 00:26:02,280
Today at its headquarters in Newark, Delaware,

466
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:06,680
Gore-Tex generates over $1 billion in sales.

467
00:26:06,681 --> 00:26:09,240
Its special material keeps water out,

468
00:26:09,620 --> 00:26:12,040
but allows humans' sweat to evaporate.

469
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:16,460
It's the perfect active outdoor material,

470
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:19,940
and it was discovered out of frustration.

471
00:26:20,620 --> 00:26:22,720
In the early days of the material,

472
00:26:23,240 --> 00:26:27,180
Bob Gore was attempting to get more out of the material itself

473
00:26:27,181 --> 00:26:30,160
by moving it and stretching it at very, very slow rates.

474
00:26:30,680 --> 00:26:33,740
And one night out of frustration, the discovery itself occurred,

475
00:26:33,741 --> 00:26:36,620
and that was that if it was pooled very, very quickly,

476
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:38,880
that took on this completely new character.

477
00:26:39,360 --> 00:26:40,660
Gore-Tex had been discovered.

478
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:43,920
Its secret ingredient, called PTFE,

479
00:26:44,160 --> 00:26:47,000
was the key to Gore-Tex's breathable brand of waterproofing.

480
00:26:47,340 --> 00:26:49,520
PTFE is the same as Teflon.

481
00:26:49,640 --> 00:26:51,260
Well, it all starts with this rock,

482
00:26:51,320 --> 00:26:53,020
and inside this rock, there's fluorine.

483
00:26:53,460 --> 00:26:56,140
Fluorine is the magical ingredient that gets us started.

484
00:26:56,940 --> 00:27:00,400
From that base material, we're then able to turn that

485
00:27:00,401 --> 00:27:03,100
through a series of reactions into this very fine powder,

486
00:27:03,101 --> 00:27:06,980
and this fine powder is then processed from that form

487
00:27:06,981 --> 00:27:11,180
into what is ultimately a full-density plastic.

488
00:27:11,760 --> 00:27:13,560
The next step is heating.

489
00:27:14,140 --> 00:27:16,920
If we take this full-density PTFE out of the oven

490
00:27:16,921 --> 00:27:20,680
and we were to just pull it very, very slowly,

491
00:27:21,220 --> 00:27:23,600
we see we run into a lot of resistance.

492
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:26,280
It's very, very hard to pull it.

493
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:29,980
However, if we take the same material

494
00:27:29,981 --> 00:27:33,780
and we grab onto either end and we pull it fast,

495
00:27:34,420 --> 00:27:37,620
what we see ultimately is we've created a completely new material.

496
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:43,740
And what was very, very stiff is now very, very soft.

497
00:27:44,280 --> 00:27:45,860
And what we've essentially done here

498
00:27:45,861 --> 00:27:47,540
is we've turned a full-density structure

499
00:27:47,541 --> 00:27:50,860
into something with millions and millions of tiny pores.

500
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:54,740
It's those millions of tiny pores

501
00:27:54,741 --> 00:27:56,960
that give Gore-Tex its unique dual action

502
00:27:56,961 --> 00:27:57,960
when it comes to moisture.

503
00:27:58,960 --> 00:28:02,340
The PTFE pores are 20,000 times smaller

504
00:28:02,341 --> 00:28:06,060
than a water droplet, yet 700 times larger

505
00:28:06,061 --> 00:28:07,220
than water vapor.

506
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:12,360
So they keep rainwater out while allowing sweat to escape.

507
00:28:13,760 --> 00:28:16,220
This waterproof yet breathable membrane

508
00:28:16,221 --> 00:28:18,660
can be added as a layer to all sorts of clothes.

509
00:28:20,820 --> 00:28:23,140
Gore-Tex routinely puts its garments to the test.

510
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:26,700
So at this point, we're head-to-toe Gore-Tex.

511
00:28:26,701 --> 00:28:29,360
We've got a Gore-Tex jacket, we've got Gore-Tex pants,

512
00:28:29,361 --> 00:28:31,100
and Gore-Tex shoes.

513
00:28:31,101 --> 00:28:34,500
The ultimate test is in a watery torture chamber,

514
00:28:34,501 --> 00:28:36,140
the rain room.

515
00:28:36,141 --> 00:28:38,340
The room generates overhead rain

516
00:28:38,341 --> 00:28:40,840
at the rate of 3 inches an hour.

517
00:28:40,841 --> 00:28:43,580
To simulate riding a motorcycle in a storm,

518
00:28:43,581 --> 00:28:47,180
it's also fitted with a nozzle spraying directly in the face

519
00:28:47,181 --> 00:28:50,400
at 22 inches an hour.

520
00:28:50,401 --> 00:28:52,400
Here we have Hannah, who wanted to bring her own jacket

521
00:28:52,401 --> 00:28:54,500
into the rain room to see what would happen.

522
00:28:54,501 --> 00:28:56,540
Now, this is a typical nylon jacket

523
00:28:56,541 --> 00:28:58,580
without a Gore-Tex membrane in it.

524
00:28:58,581 --> 00:29:00,000
So we're going to see what happens.

525
00:29:13,220 --> 00:29:14,560
How's that Seattle spirit?

526
00:29:14,561 --> 00:29:15,980
It's great.

527
00:29:15,981 --> 00:29:17,020
You look a little wet.

528
00:29:17,021 --> 00:29:18,820
I am a little wet, actually.

529
00:29:18,821 --> 00:29:19,820
Oh.

530
00:29:19,821 --> 00:29:24,820
So yeah, definitely not waterproof.

531
00:29:24,821 --> 00:29:27,820
As you can see, the back actually got pretty wet, too.

532
00:29:27,821 --> 00:29:29,820
I'm actually feeling pretty dry.

533
00:29:33,820 --> 00:29:34,820
You're not wet.

534
00:29:34,821 --> 00:29:37,820
No, warm, dry, comfortable.

535
00:29:37,821 --> 00:29:38,820
I'm sure you are.

536
00:29:38,821 --> 00:29:39,720
I'm sure you are.

537
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:51,400
Your home is your castle, and the battle against water

538
00:29:51,401 --> 00:29:56,700
is taking place all around you, in every corner of your house.

539
00:29:56,701 --> 00:29:59,500
You just may not know it.

540
00:29:59,501 --> 00:30:04,140
Groundwater may be looking for a way into your basement.

541
00:30:04,141 --> 00:30:08,320
Mold may be clandestinely taking over your walls.

542
00:30:08,321 --> 00:30:13,900
A broken shingle may be the weak link in your line of defense.

543
00:30:13,901 --> 00:30:17,320
Each year, thousands of homes suffer from hidden water

544
00:30:17,321 --> 00:30:20,540
damage.

545
00:30:20,541 --> 00:30:25,180
Here in Canton, Ohio, Victory Homes president James Rudeau

546
00:30:25,181 --> 00:30:27,660
has been overseeing the hammering and sawing of his crew

547
00:30:27,661 --> 00:30:30,220
since dawn.

548
00:30:30,221 --> 00:30:32,080
They know that rainwater and mold

549
00:30:32,081 --> 00:30:35,060
can slowly destroy their work down the road,

550
00:30:35,061 --> 00:30:37,500
so they're marshaling the best defenses available

551
00:30:37,501 --> 00:30:38,500
to builders today.

552
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:51,180
Like a hat, the roof is the first line of defense

553
00:30:51,181 --> 00:30:53,040
against rain.

554
00:30:53,041 --> 00:30:55,920
It channels the water away from the house.

555
00:30:55,921 --> 00:30:58,180
A drainage plane is crucial when we're designing a house.

556
00:30:58,181 --> 00:30:59,280
We don't want a flat roof.

557
00:30:59,281 --> 00:31:01,160
We want to be able to have peaks and valleys.

558
00:31:01,161 --> 00:31:03,460
Just like the home behind me, where the two roofs come

559
00:31:03,461 --> 00:31:04,860
together is called a valley.

560
00:31:04,861 --> 00:31:07,160
When water is shed down the roof, it goes into the valley,

561
00:31:07,161 --> 00:31:08,520
which drains into the gutter system.

562
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:10,520
In addition to peaks and valleys,

563
00:31:10,940 --> 00:31:13,100
the shingles themselves take their cue

564
00:31:13,101 --> 00:31:14,800
from the feathers of a bird.

565
00:31:14,980 --> 00:31:16,400
They all kind of overlap each other,

566
00:31:16,460 --> 00:31:17,640
so then when we start the next row,

567
00:31:17,960 --> 00:31:19,500
this seam then would be in the center

568
00:31:19,501 --> 00:31:21,360
or off to the side of that other shingle.

569
00:31:21,460 --> 00:31:23,780
And as we go up, it all feathers in naturally.

570
00:31:24,900 --> 00:31:27,160
Today's technique of overlapping slates

571
00:31:27,161 --> 00:31:29,420
actually dates back 2,000 years.

572
00:31:29,940 --> 00:31:32,380
The Romans introduced this style of roof to Great Britain.

573
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:34,420
But the cheaper thatched roof

574
00:31:34,421 --> 00:31:36,660
remained more popular until the 12th century.

575
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:40,200
when King John issued a law that London citizens

576
00:31:40,300 --> 00:31:43,100
had to replace their roofs with clay tiles

577
00:31:43,200 --> 00:31:45,200
in order to prevent fires.

578
00:31:45,300 --> 00:31:47,100
This marked the beginning of mass-produced

579
00:31:47,200 --> 00:31:51,300
industrial roofing methods.

580
00:31:51,400 --> 00:31:54,320
Home builders call modern waterproofing technology

581
00:31:54,420 --> 00:31:57,720
vapor barriers because they block moisture.

582
00:31:57,820 --> 00:32:01,020
One of the most common vapor barriers is house wrap,

583
00:32:01,120 --> 00:32:03,260
a synthetic polyethylene paper.

584
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:04,520
It stays dry.

585
00:32:04,620 --> 00:32:05,720
What's great and unique about the paper

586
00:32:05,721 --> 00:32:07,560
is that it doesn't, it's not a sponge.

587
00:32:07,561 --> 00:32:08,720
It can't absorb water.

588
00:32:08,721 --> 00:32:10,660
It's almost like putting a big plastic fish tank

589
00:32:10,661 --> 00:32:12,760
over top of your house and having it water hit it.

590
00:32:12,761 --> 00:32:15,160
It's going to just drain right down it.

591
00:32:15,161 --> 00:32:18,160
The surface of the house wrap is hydrophobic,

592
00:32:18,161 --> 00:32:19,860
like wax paper.

593
00:32:19,861 --> 00:32:22,060
That means it's not wettable.

594
00:32:22,061 --> 00:32:26,480
Something that's wettable is also called hydrophilic.

595
00:32:26,481 --> 00:32:30,020
That is to say, loves water or would adhere to water.

596
00:32:30,021 --> 00:32:33,380
The opposite of hydrophilic is hydrophobic.

597
00:32:33,381 --> 00:32:38,280
The water pulls away from that surface and doesn't wet it.

598
00:32:38,380 --> 00:32:40,620
Builders use house wrap to transform wood

599
00:32:40,720 --> 00:32:44,920
from hydrophilic to hydrophobic.

600
00:32:45,020 --> 00:32:46,720
But just how does house wrap work?

601
00:32:51,440 --> 00:32:54,140
Here at DuPont's factory in Richmond, Virginia,

602
00:32:54,240 --> 00:32:56,440
Tyvek house wrap is being put to the test.

603
00:32:59,240 --> 00:33:02,240
But house wrap is not just about keeping water out.

604
00:33:02,241 --> 00:33:05,980
The wrap also allows the house to breathe.

605
00:33:06,080 --> 00:33:08,820
Just by living in a house, cooking, doing laundry,

606
00:33:08,920 --> 00:33:11,620
taking showers, even breathing, we

607
00:33:11,720 --> 00:33:15,820
can generate three to six gallons of water vapor a day.

608
00:33:15,920 --> 00:33:18,160
If that moisture vapor were allowed to accumulate

609
00:33:18,260 --> 00:33:20,400
in the wall, it could cause damage.

610
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:23,720
And what house wraps allow is for that moisture vapor

611
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:27,060
to leave the wall system so that that damage doesn't occur.

612
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:32,000
Tyvek house wrap is made from very fine

613
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:35,840
high-density polyethylene fibers in a special process

614
00:33:35,900 --> 00:33:37,680
called flash spinning.

615
00:33:37,740 --> 00:33:41,000
When we make house wrap, we lay fiber on top of fiber

616
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:42,780
to create a fabric.

617
00:33:42,840 --> 00:33:44,480
Unlike tarp, which is laminated

618
00:33:44,540 --> 00:33:47,520
with a waterproof coating, Tyvek fibers are heat

619
00:33:47,580 --> 00:33:49,560
and pressure bonded to each other.

620
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:52,460
Because we lay those fibers on top of each other,

621
00:33:52,520 --> 00:33:56,000
we create minuscule air pockets that allow moisture vapor

622
00:33:56,060 --> 00:33:57,520
to escape.

623
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:59,360
When moisture can escape,

624
00:33:59,420 --> 00:34:02,000
it prevents a very old problem.

625
00:34:02,001 --> 00:34:03,960
Mold.

626
00:34:04,060 --> 00:34:07,840
Mold infestations have plagued society for thousands of years.

627
00:34:07,940 --> 00:34:09,540
It's even mentioned in the Old Testament.

628
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:14,640
Mold can grow anywhere there's moisture,

629
00:34:14,740 --> 00:34:18,320
even on inorganic material like concrete or paint,

630
00:34:18,420 --> 00:34:21,660
so long as it has dust or soil to live off.

631
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:25,060
Recent concerns about sick buildings and killer mold

632
00:34:25,160 --> 00:34:27,660
have pushed forward methods for waterproofing homes.

633
00:34:27,661 --> 00:34:30,660
homes.

634
00:34:30,760 --> 00:34:32,960
Because of how it's built, a basement

635
00:34:33,060 --> 00:34:35,000
can absorb large amounts of moisture,

636
00:34:35,100 --> 00:34:37,000
where mold can thrive.

637
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:38,500
Concrete block is an absorption.

638
00:34:38,600 --> 00:34:41,400
It'll soak in like a sponge, and it's called wicking.

639
00:34:41,500 --> 00:34:43,700
This will actually wick and start making the block walls

640
00:34:43,800 --> 00:34:46,780
inside the basement wet.

641
00:34:46,880 --> 00:34:48,820
To prevent foundations from wicking,

642
00:34:48,920 --> 00:34:52,260
workers apply a liquid rubber membrane.

643
00:34:52,360 --> 00:34:54,760
This tar-like substance will adhere to the next layer

644
00:34:54,761 --> 00:34:58,800
layer of skin, the insulation board.

645
00:34:58,801 --> 00:35:03,560
This is another way to channel water away from the house.

646
00:35:03,561 --> 00:35:06,600
The boards ensure that any water that gets near the wall

647
00:35:06,601 --> 00:35:10,880
will be directed down to what's called a French drain.

648
00:35:10,881 --> 00:35:13,820
This has holes in it, hole space down.

649
00:35:13,821 --> 00:35:16,420
We're giving the water a path of least resistance to run.

650
00:35:16,421 --> 00:35:17,480
We have gravel underneath.

651
00:35:17,481 --> 00:35:20,920
We cover this with gravel on top.

652
00:35:20,921 --> 00:35:23,460
And that way, any water that runs along our vapor barriers,

653
00:35:23,461 --> 00:35:26,300
from the top of the roof all the way down to the foundation

654
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:28,100
is gonna find a path of loose resistance,

655
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:30,200
which is this pipe.

656
00:35:30,300 --> 00:35:31,740
And this all drains into a sump pump,

657
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:34,100
which gets pumped out into our storm drains.

658
00:35:34,200 --> 00:35:36,140
Advances in waterproofing homes

659
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:38,940
give us new weapons in the battle for dryness.

660
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:47,320
Water is a part of us, 60% to be exact.

661
00:35:47,420 --> 00:35:50,580
Yet we, too, are ingeniously waterproof.

662
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:53,440
Our skin repels water, but lets you

663
00:35:53,460 --> 00:35:56,300
moisture escape from inside.

664
00:35:56,301 --> 00:36:00,560
Humans and animals have natural waterproofing with your skin.

665
00:36:00,561 --> 00:36:03,060
If you didn't, going swimming or going out in the rain

666
00:36:03,061 --> 00:36:06,660
would be a very dangerous proposition.

667
00:36:06,661 --> 00:36:08,620
Plants and animals have similar strategies

668
00:36:08,621 --> 00:36:12,100
for controlling evaporation and hydration.

669
00:36:12,101 --> 00:36:15,620
Many birds have a special gland that produces oil.

670
00:36:15,621 --> 00:36:19,000
The oil keeps the feathers waterproof and buoyant.

671
00:36:19,001 --> 00:36:22,220
Sea otters keep warm and dry with two layers of fur.

672
00:36:22,221 --> 00:36:24,420
The outer hairs are long and waterproof,

673
00:36:24,421 --> 00:36:28,120
protecting a thicker, shorter layer underneath.

674
00:36:28,121 --> 00:36:32,100
In an arid region of Argentina, the Argentinian monkey frog

675
00:36:32,101 --> 00:36:35,500
secretes wax from specialized glands in its skin

676
00:36:35,501 --> 00:36:38,940
to form a barrier against water loss.

677
00:36:38,941 --> 00:36:40,700
The frogs are waterproofing their skin

678
00:36:40,701 --> 00:36:41,980
with their own blend of wax.

679
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:48,780
Waxes are a combination of fatty acids and alcohols.

680
00:36:48,781 --> 00:36:53,320
They usually melt at temperatures above 113 degrees Fahrenheit.

681
00:36:53,321 --> 00:36:56,780
Because they're insoluble, waxes provide us yet another way

682
00:36:56,781 --> 00:36:57,620
to waterproof.

683
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:03,000
It wasn't long after Henry Ford figured out how

684
00:37:03,001 --> 00:37:06,500
to mass produce the automobile that people began applying wax

685
00:37:06,501 --> 00:37:09,100
to their cars.

686
00:37:09,101 --> 00:37:12,640
Simon Eyes, named after its founder, George Simons,

687
00:37:12,641 --> 00:37:16,180
began mass producing car wax in 1911.

688
00:37:16,181 --> 00:37:18,940
You can now find Simonize in the dictionary,

689
00:37:18,941 --> 00:37:22,720
defined as to shine or polish to a high sheen,

690
00:37:22,721 --> 00:37:24,560
especially with wax.

691
00:37:24,561 --> 00:37:27,560
Simonize wax protection against road chemicals,

692
00:37:27,561 --> 00:37:29,860
fumes, rust, corrosion, and that's

693
00:37:29,861 --> 00:37:32,260
of shiny cars and glamour.

694
00:37:32,261 --> 00:37:35,800
Give your car Simonize wax protection.

695
00:37:35,801 --> 00:37:36,800
Sorry, old girl.

696
00:37:36,801 --> 00:37:39,200
Wrong button.

697
00:37:39,201 --> 00:37:41,140
But there's a science behind it,

698
00:37:41,141 --> 00:37:44,380
and it has to do with the way wax molecules behave.

699
00:37:44,381 --> 00:37:47,920
One end of the molecule adheres to the paint.

700
00:37:47,921 --> 00:37:50,820
The other end that now is shown to the world

701
00:37:50,821 --> 00:37:53,620
is something that the water doesn't want to stick to.

702
00:37:53,621 --> 00:37:55,900
This is how you know you have good wax jugs,

703
00:37:55,901 --> 00:37:57,860
when the water beads up on it.

704
00:37:57,861 --> 00:38:00,140
Wax is wonderfully water repellent,

705
00:38:00,141 --> 00:38:03,360
but it doesn't stay on cars for more than a few months.

706
00:38:03,361 --> 00:38:06,400
For car owners, the holy grail has been a coating

707
00:38:06,401 --> 00:38:08,440
that would permanently protect their car

708
00:38:08,441 --> 00:38:11,400
from all kinds of harsh elements.

709
00:38:11,401 --> 00:38:13,740
Here at headquarters in Bolton, Connecticut,

710
00:38:13,741 --> 00:38:17,140
The Simon Eyes company believes they found a unique waterproofing product,

711
00:38:18,920 --> 00:38:19,620
glass coat.

712
00:38:20,020 --> 00:38:22,660
Because it doesn't need to be reapplied every few months,

713
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:25,060
they call it the anti-wax.

714
00:38:25,820 --> 00:38:27,860
Chemist David White invented glass coat

715
00:38:27,861 --> 00:38:30,740
after conducting endless experiments on his own car.

716
00:38:32,420 --> 00:38:37,060
I probably went through five or six hundred formulations,

717
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:39,800
and I just kept grinding away at it,

718
00:38:39,801 --> 00:38:42,160
zeroing in on the precise application,

719
00:38:42,161 --> 00:38:45,600
and then it didn't do anything that it shouldn't have done.

720
00:38:45,640 --> 00:38:48,140
It did everything it should have done, and I said, aha.

721
00:38:49,240 --> 00:38:51,300
Simon Eyes closely guards their secret,

722
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:54,480
but the key ingredient is a chemical compound

723
00:38:54,481 --> 00:38:56,100
called polysilazane.

724
00:38:56,840 --> 00:38:59,720
Its waterproof properties make it an effective coating

725
00:38:59,721 --> 00:39:04,240
for bridges, solar panels, and tiles on NASA's space shuttles.

726
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:09,080
Glass Coat takes the useful properties of polysilazane

727
00:39:09,081 --> 00:39:10,760
into the world of cars.

728
00:39:10,761 --> 00:39:13,400
Polysilazane reacts with hydrogen and oxygen,

729
00:39:13,500 --> 00:39:16,440
and it forms microns of glass on top of the clear coat.

730
00:39:16,540 --> 00:39:18,040
And those microns of glass are what

731
00:39:18,140 --> 00:39:20,540
help keep this product very hydrophobic

732
00:39:20,640 --> 00:39:22,900
and also protects it for a long period of time

733
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:25,240
against tree sap, bird waste, insects,

734
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:27,880
anything that can eat the clear coat.

735
00:39:27,980 --> 00:39:30,920
Polysilazane is a compound made of silicon nitrogen

736
00:39:31,020 --> 00:39:32,520
molecules.

737
00:39:32,620 --> 00:39:34,480
The nitrogen reacts with water vapor

738
00:39:34,580 --> 00:39:38,180
to catalyze a bond between silicon and oxygen.

739
00:39:38,280 --> 00:39:40,220
When the silicon and oxygen come together,

740
00:39:40,221 --> 00:39:43,760
they form a super hard glass, which acts as an effective

741
00:39:43,761 --> 00:39:46,360
barrier to the many enemies of car surfaces,

742
00:39:46,361 --> 00:39:49,220
from a car wash to acid rain.

743
00:39:49,221 --> 00:39:52,460
Acid rain is formed by particulates up in the air

744
00:39:52,461 --> 00:39:54,860
that are typically sulfur dioxide.

745
00:39:54,861 --> 00:39:57,340
The water falls down to the hard surface, in this case,

746
00:39:57,341 --> 00:39:58,640
the paint of a vehicle.

747
00:39:58,641 --> 00:40:01,640
And when it evaporates, the perimeter of the water drop

748
00:40:01,641 --> 00:40:05,640
heats up and it puts an acid etch on the car.

749
00:40:05,641 --> 00:40:07,920
With the application of glass coat,

750
00:40:07,921 --> 00:40:10,920
Simon Eyes guarantees protection of a vehicle's top coat

751
00:40:10,921 --> 00:40:12,920
for seven years.

752
00:40:12,921 --> 00:40:15,920
So you've got the base metal, you have a primer,

753
00:40:15,921 --> 00:40:18,920
you have the color paint, you have the clear coat on top of that.

754
00:40:18,921 --> 00:40:20,920
Glass Coat goes on top of that and protects the entire system

755
00:40:20,921 --> 00:40:22,920
underneath.

756
00:40:22,921 --> 00:40:24,920
Glass Coat's waterproof properties have been tested

757
00:40:24,921 --> 00:40:27,920
by Simon Eyes and university researchers.

758
00:40:27,921 --> 00:40:30,920
After applying Glass Coat to only one side of the hood,

759
00:40:30,921 --> 00:40:34,920
they ran the car through an industrial wash a hundred times.

760
00:40:37,920 --> 00:40:41,420
The half without the protection was wet after the wash,

761
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:44,960
but the other side sheaths or sheds the water

762
00:40:45,060 --> 00:40:47,660
so that it beads up and rolls off,

763
00:40:47,760 --> 00:40:50,860
a practical application of cutting-edge waterproofing

764
00:40:50,960 --> 00:40:51,660
science.

765
00:40:54,060 --> 00:40:56,980
These days, waterproofing isn't just for mountain climbers

766
00:40:57,080 --> 00:40:58,480
or firefighters.

767
00:40:58,580 --> 00:41:01,480
Now it can go poolside.

768
00:41:01,580 --> 00:41:04,420
Objects that used to be completely destroyed by water

769
00:41:04,520 --> 00:41:05,980
are now water-friendly.

